  "strategy": "Approach and Rationale:\nI propose a multi-phased quantum-inspired optimization that combines evolutionary search with explicit length control and periodic restarts. This approach differs from previous ones by using a population-based search with quantum-inspired crossover/mutation in addition to traditional simplex optimization, while maintaining awareness of sequence length and dimensions. It should explore different sequence representations and leverage dimensionality for breaking through local optima bottlenecks.\n\nUse a two-stage framework:\nPhase 1: Quantum-inspired iterative refinement (strong crossover/mutation)\nPhase 2: Dimension-aware restarts and length perturbation\n\nComponents to include:\n1. Performance tracking over iterations while balancing evaluation cost\n2. Maintain multiple \"blocks\" of candidates to record historical optima\n3. Explicit length-based dimensions separate from parent initialization\n4. Dynamic switching between standalone simplex projection and length-resampling\n5. Quantum-inspired crossover/mutation as secondary evolution mechanism\n6. Scheduled evaluation intervals to validate candidates against the exact objective\n\nMathematical Specification:\n- Sequences represented as probability/frequency vectors (nonnegative, potentially over unit sum)\n- Maintains explicit length (n) as an evolution parameter\n- Evaluates fitness using 2*n*max(convolve(a, a)) / (sum(a))^2 (the full spectrum minimization)\n- Implements quantum-inspired states by mixing probabilistic and deterministic components:\n\n```\n1. Promotion system based on quantum fidelity:\n   fidelity = 1 - |current_state - reference_state\u1d40|\n   promotes sequences converging toward optimal patterns\n\n2. Length preserving crossover (\u03c3_interval-based):\n   \u03c3_n = standard deviation of scores for the current length\n   crossover_range = [0.8 * \u03c3_n, 1.2 * \u03c3_n]\n   targets lengths with favorable standard deviation\n\n3. Mutually orthogonal simplex projection dimensions:\n   for each dimension d, project into simplex with basis vectors orthogonal to common patterns\n\n4. Nonnegative-uniform mutation with adaptive neighborhood size\"\"\"\n</strategy>\nImplementation Procedure:\n```python\nsteps=50\nbest_candidate = raw_input_parent_sequence (clamped to [0,1000] and normalized)\nmilestones = []\nfor i in range(1, int(budget_s/100)+1):\n    milestones.append(100*i*budget_scale)\n\ndef evolve_ensemble():\n    \"\"\"Maintains three orthogonal ensemble views: \n    full simplex projection, cross-validated dimensions, \n    and quantum-inspired fidelity weights.\"\"\"\n    global best_candidate, current_seq\n    \n    # Quantum crossover with dynamic partner selection\n    overlap_scores = cross_validate_scores()\n    top_ensemble_scores = get_top_scores(quantum_subsample=overlap_scores)\n    # Dynamic quantum crossover between top performers\n    crossover_0 = random_state_evolution(top_ensemble_scores, quantum_fidelity=0.8)\n    crossover_1 = crossover_0[:]\n    crossover_2 = crossover_0[:]\n    \n    # Standard mutation applied\n    std = current_std / 3\n    mutation = best_candidate + np.random.normal(0, std, len(best_candidate))\n    \n    # Blend quantum aspects with classical mutation\n    quantum_decay = time_phase / budget_s\n    crossover_0 = crossover_0 * (1-quantum_decay) + mutation * quantum_decay\n    \n    # Track improvements across milestones\n    current_fitness = evaluate_fitness(crossover_0)\n    update_orthogonal_components(crossover_0)\n\ndef periodic_restart():\n    \"\"\"Re-trains in a different dimension space, preserving evolution key.\"\"\"\n    # Force normalize current sequence\n    new_dimension = current_length * dimension_perturbation()\n    new_seq = resample_sequence(best_candidate, new_length)\n    \n    # Re-start with angular momentum preservation\n    evolve_angular_momentum(new_seq)\n    \n    # Blend with existing best_candidate\n    blend_weight = get_periodic_blend